Subject: [PATCH 02637/11362] [conductor] In-process docs for PHASE C
 (CONTINUOUS SELF-LEARNING; mandated per research-program.md;
 verifier_is_oracle=false) -- does ACTIVE-LEARNING trace selection produce
 COMPOUNDING where the .406 SIZE-only growth SATURATED? (ActPRM
 arXiv:2504.10559). .406 (exp4396) grew the first-error corpus by SIZE alone
 and saturated (learning curve F1=1.0 FLAT 566->5661,
 localizer_compounds=false) -- but that was on the ARTIFACT-confounded
 synthetic localizer (no real headroom). The NEW mechanism: ACTIVE selection
 -- pick the next traces to label by UNCERTAINTY (the localizer's
 least-confident first-error predictions) + first-error-POSITION DIVERSITY
 (cover under-represented positions, NOT just add more of the same), and
 measure held-out first-error F1 vs the accumulated-corpus SIZE as an
 ACTIVE-vs-RANDOM learning curve. Run on the BEST available first-error
 localizer: exp4403's REAL-intervention localizer if it is genuine, ELSE the
 deconfounded real-intervention corpus from exp4403 (the question is whether
 ACTIVE selection breaks the saturation that SIZE-only growth could not). Keep
 the template-family holdout + a content-blind position-only control IN the
 active loop (so 'compounding' is not re-learning the artifact). Emit
 localizer_compounds BARE bool := (held-out first-error F1 under ACTIVE
 selection RISES with corpus size BEYOND a RANDOM-selection baseline at the
 same corpus size, delta CI95-excl-0, with a positive control confirming
 non-trivial ceiling headroom). verifier_is_oracle=false. A win = the
 localizer COMPOUNDS via active selection (the self-learning headline on the
 axis that now has real headroom); a clean null (active selection does not
 beat random -> the localizer's signal is saturated or position-bound) is
 decision-grade. Cached candidates, CPU, zero quota. NO TRM training.

